<p>Three consequences:</p>
<ul>
<li>A struct, a tuple and a fixed-size array are values: the whole value sits in the frame, and assigning it makes another whole value.</li>
<li>A <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> or <code>StrBuf</code> is a small header in the frame plus a buffer elsewhere, with a single owner.</li>
<li>The buffer's place depends on the <em>current allocator</em>, not the type: the same <code>List</code> code puts it on the heap in one place and in an arena in another.</li>
</ul>
<p>This program touches each place once; read the comments, then the output.</p>
<example name="book-13-places"/>
<p><code>stackalloc i64[4]</code> reserves four frame slots, freed when the scope ends, so a borrow of them cannot leave it. Bounds checks apply in every build mode: indexing past the end stops the program with <code>panic: index out of range</code> and the line number, never reading stray memory.</p>

<h2 id="copy">Copies are values, lists are owned</h2>
<p>A struct is a value, so <code>var q = p</code> makes a second, independent value, and a fixed-size array works the same way. A <code>List</code> owns a buffer, so Bayan never copies it for you.</p>
<example name="book-13-copies"/>
<p>Changing <code>q</code> did not change <code>p</code>, and the array behaves the same. The list copy is explicit: <code>xs.copy()</code> allocates a new buffer from the current allocator and copies the elements; <code>xs.copy(to: arena)</code> allocates from the allocator you name.</p>
<p>Now the copy you might write without thinking, with no <code>.copy()</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    xs.append(1)
    var ys = xs
    ys.append(2)
    print($"{xs.length}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> copy-list.byn:4:14

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     var ys = <span class="hl">xs</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> hand the value over with `move xs`; make an independent copy with `xs.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
<span class="gut">    │ 4 │</span>     var ys = <span class="add">move </span>xs
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>              <span class="add">+++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>copy-list.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>A type that owns storage is <em>unique</em>: one owner at a time. Every <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>Set</code> and <code>StrBuf</code> is unique, as is a struct with a unique field. The error names the type, says why, and offers three fixes; pick by what you mean:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>move xs</code> hands the list to <code>ys</code> and leaves <code>xs</code> gone.</li>
<li><code>xs.copy(to: alloc)</code> makes two independent lists.</li>
<li><code>ref</code> shares the one list for the duration of a call.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="move">Moving ownership</h2>
<p><code>move</code> hands a value to a new owner, written where the hand-over happens: in a <code>let</code>, in a struct literal, and in a call whose parameter is declared <code>move</code>.</p>
<example name="book-13-moves"/>
<p><code>let b = move a</code> gives the list to <code>b</code>; the struct literal moves <code>b</code> into <code>batch.items</code>. <code>batch.items.take()</code> moves the list <em>out</em> of the field and leaves an empty list, so <code>left</code> is 0. <code>total(move items)</code> gives the list to a function declaring <code>move List&lt;i64&gt; xs</code>, which owns and frees it when it returns.</p>
<p>Only a local variable or a <code>move</code> parameter can be moved from: moving out of a field or an element would leave a half-emptied value, so use <code>take()</code> there.</p>
<p>After a move the old name is dead; using it is an error that points at the move:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    xs.append(1)
    let b = move xs
    print($"{xs.length}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2781:</span> `xs` is used after it was moved
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> use-after-move.byn:5:14

<span class="gut">  5 │</span>     print($"{<span class="hl">xs</span>.length}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^^ moved on line 4</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> assign `xs` a new value before using it again, or move a `.copy(to: alloc)` of it instead

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2781</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>use-after-move.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The help line fixes it: assign a new value, or move a copy.</p>

<h2 id="borrow">Borrowing without owning</h2>
<p>Most code only reads a list or changes it for a moment; a borrow does that without taking ownership. Four forms:</p>
<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>Form</th><th>What the function may do</th></tr>
<tr><td>A plain parameter, <code>List&lt;i64&gt; xs</code></td><td>Read the list; it cannot change it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt;</code></td><td>Read a run of elements from a list, an array or another span.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ref List&lt;i64&gt;</code></td><td>Change the list; the caller writes <code>ref xs</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A slice, <code>xs.span[1..]</code>, or a string slice such as <code>text[0..5]</code></td><td>View part of something.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<example name="book-13-borrows"/>
<p>Nothing is copied here: <code>sum</code> sees a view, <code>doubleAll</code> changes the one list through <code>ref</code>, and <code>count</code> reads it.</p>

<h3 id="borrow-rules">The rules a borrow follows</h3>
<p>The compiler infers every borrow's lifetime from what it points into. Three rules:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>You cannot change a value while a borrow of it is in use:</strong> an append can move the buffer and leave the view pointing at freed memory.</li>
<li><strong>A borrow cannot outlive what it points into.</strong> You cannot return a view of a local, or keep a view of an arena after the arena is gone.</li>
<li><strong>Literals and <code>const</code> data are static</strong> and can be borrowed anywhere.</li>
</ol>
<p>Rule 1: <code>first</code> views <code>xs</code> and is used again after the <code>append</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    xs.append(1)
    let first = xs.span
    xs.append(2)
    print($"{first[0]}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3004:</span> `xs` is modified while `first` still borrows it
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> modify-borrowed.byn:5:5

<span class="gut">  5 │</span>     <span class="hl">xs.append(2)</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^ `first` is used again on line 6</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> finish using `first` first, or copy what it needs before changing `xs`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3004</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>modify-borrowed.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The caret names the later use. Finish with <code>first</code> before the <code>append</code>, or keep the value you need (<code>let first = xs[0]</code>); moving the <code>print</code> up is enough, because the compiler looks at the last <em>use</em> of a borrow, not the end of the block.</p>
<p>A <code>for</code> loop borrows what it walks; here a method that changes <code>cart</code> runs inside the loop over <code>cart.lines</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Cart {
    List&lt;i64&gt; lines

    void add(ref this, i64 x) {
        lines.append(x)
    }
}

void main() {
    var cart = Cart { lines: List&lt;i64&gt;() }
    cart.add(1)
    for line in cart.lines {
        cart.add(line)
    }
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3004:</span> `cart` is modified while the `for` loop still borrows it
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> loop-borrow.byn:13:9

<span class="gut">  13 │</span>         <span class="hl">cart.add(line)</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>         <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the loop over `cart` is still running</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> collect the changes in another list and apply them to `cart` after the loop

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3004</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>loop-borrow.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Borrows are tracked per variable, not per field: the call changes <code>cart</code> as a whole, so it conflicts with a borrow of <code>cart.lines</code>. Collect the changes in another list and apply them after the loop.</p>

<h2 id="arenas">Arenas: free many things at once</h2>
<p>A growing list keeps asking the allocator for room and frees its own buffer when its owner goes away. When many small allocations die together, an arena is cheaper: it hands out memory, and you free it all in one step.</p>
<p>Two forms:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>using var arena = Arena()</code> makes an arena freed at the end of the current scope.</li>
<li><code>provide allocator: arena { … }</code> makes it the current allocator for the block and every function the block calls; you do not pass it as an argument.</li>
</ul>
<example name="book-13-arena-batches"/>
<p><code>words</code> knows nothing of arenas: it builds a <code>List</code> with <code>uses alloc</code> (<a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>), and the buffer comes from the current allocator. Inside the block it is the arena, which is freed at the end of each loop pass together with every list made in it. <code>longest</code> is a plain value, so it safely leaves the block; a list or string made inside would not.</p>
<p>That is rule 2: copy a string into an arena and keep it past the arena's scope:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var kept = ""
    {
        using let arena = Arena()
        kept = "copied".copy(to: arena)
    }
    print(kept)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3002:</span> arena `arena` does not live as long as `kept`, which borrows it
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> arena-escape.byn:5:16

<span class="gut">  5 │</span>         kept = <span class="hl">"copied".copy(to: arena)</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>                <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ arena `arena` is freed at the end of its block</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> copy the data with `.copy(to: alloc)` into an allocator that lives long enough

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3002</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>arena-escape.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The fix is an allocator that outlives the data: <code>.copy(to: alloc)</code> into an outer arena or the default allocator. A function cannot return an arena-backed string either: the arena dies when it returns:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">string label() {
    using var arena = Arena()
    let s = "abc".copy(to: arena)
    return s
}

void main() {
    print(label())
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3003:</span> cannot return a borrow of arena `arena`, which is freed when `label` returns
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> return-arena.byn:4:12

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     return <span class="hl">s</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>            <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> let the caller pass the buffer or allocator in, and borrow or allocate from that

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3003</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>return-arena.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The usual shape lets the caller decide: the caller makes the arena (or not), wraps the call in <code>provide allocator: …</code>, and the function allocates from the current allocator and returns the result. <code>arena.reset()</code> frees everything in an arena but keeps it for reuse.</p>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Pool and handle allocation, a <code>borrows</code> clause on functions, and <code>unsafe</code> blocks are described in the specification but are not available today.</note>

<h2 id="build">Putting it together</h2>
<p>How the pieces choose for a real task:</p>
<div class="tablewrap">
<table>
<tr><th>Task</th><th>Signature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Reads a big list, returns one number</td><td>A <code>ReadOnlySpan</code> or plain <code>List</code> parameter; allocates nothing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Edits a list</td><td><code>ref</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Builds a list</td><td>Allocates from the current allocator and returns it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stores a list in a longer-lived place</td><td>Takes it as <code>move</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A batch job with lots of scratch data</td><td>Wraps each batch in an arena.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wants to keep a view of something short-lived</td><td>Copies the data it needs out.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. In <code>book-13-copies</code>, why does <code>q.x = 10</code> leave <code>p.x</code> equal to 1, while appending to <code>ys</code> leaves <code>xs.length</code> at 1 only because of <code>.copy()</code>?</summary>
<p>A struct is a value: <code>var q = p</code> makes a second whole value, independent of <code>p</code>. A <code>List</code> owns a buffer and is never copied implicitly: <code>var ys = xs</code> is BYN2780, while <code>.copy()</code> duplicates the buffer, so appending to <code>ys</code> cannot change <code>xs</code>. The output is <code>xs has 1, ys has 2</code>.</p>
<p>Sharing one list cannot happen, because the compiler refuses the assignment; "both would grow" is the aliasing bug Bayan rules out (<a href="#copy">details</a>).</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. After <code>let b = move a</code>, what does <code>print($"{a.length}")</code> do?</summary>
<p>It does not compile: BYN2781, <code>a</code> is used after it was moved. <code>a</code> is dead, not empty; the compiler rejects it before any run, and assigning <code>a = List&lt;i64&gt;()</code> makes the name usable again (<a href="#move">details</a>).</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. In <code>book-13-moves</code>, the output has the line <code>taken 3, left 0</code>. Which call produced the 0, and could you write <code>let items = move batch.items</code> instead?</summary>
<p><code>batch.items.take()</code>: it moves the list out and leaves an empty list, so <code>batch.items.length</code> is 0. <code>move batch.items</code> does not compile, because only a local or a <code>move</code> parameter can be moved from; moving out of a field would leave a half-empty struct, so <code>take()</code> is the way (<a href="#move">details</a>).</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>4. What is wrong with this loop, and what is a fix?
<pre><code class="byn">for line in cart.lines {
    cart.add(line)
}</code></pre></summary>
<p>BYN3004: the loop borrows <code>cart.lines</code> while it runs, and <code>cart.add</code> is a <code>ref this</code> method that changes <code>cart</code>. Collect the new lines in a separate list and append them after the loop; an index loop still breaks the rule while the call changes <code>cart</code>, and <code>cart.lines.copy()</code> also works at the cost of an allocation (<a href="#borrow">details</a>).</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>5. A function builds a string inside <code>provide allocator: arena { … }</code> and returns it. Why does the compiler refuse, and what are two ways out?</summary>
<p>The bytes live in the arena, freed when the function returns, so the string would point at freed memory (BYN3003). Either copy it into an allocator that outlives the call, <code>s.copy(to: alloc)</code>, or let the caller own the arena and wrap the call in <code>provide allocator</code>, so the memory lasts as long as the caller's block. <code>static</code> is no option for data built at run time: only literals and <code>const</code> data are static (<a href="#arenas">details</a>).</p></details>

